-- title:
--   p. 18 — L is locally finite, and for every ω the G_ω- and L-orbits of a ray are its cofinality class, so L is auxiliary for G_ω
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be the group of finitary automorphisms of the binary tree (`finitary`). Then:
--
--   1. $L$ is locally finite: the subgroup of `Garrido.BinaryTreeAut` generated by any finite subset of $L$ is finite;
--   2. for every string $\omega \in \{\mathbf 0, \mathbf 1, \mathbf 2\}^{\mathbb N}$ and every ray $x$, both orbits $x \cdot G_\omega$ and $x \cdot L$ (`rightOrbit`) are the cofinality class of $x$, the set of rays $y$ with $y_n = x_n$ for all large $n$;
--   3. for every $\omega$, $L$ is an auxiliary group with trivial isotropy for $G_\omega$ acting on the rays (`IsAuxiliary`): $x \cdot L = x \cdot G_\omega$ for every ray $x$, and an element of $L$ fixing a ray $x$ fixes every ray near $x$.
--
--   The string $\omega$ is arbitrary, eventually constant ones included.
--
--   Erschler and Zheng, p. 18: “Let $L$ be the subgroup of $\mathrm{Aut}(\mathsf T_{\mathbf d})$ that consists of all finitary automorphisms. In other words, an element $g \in \mathrm{Aut}(\mathsf T_{\mathbf d})$ is in $L$ if there exists a finite level $n$ such that all sections $g_v$ for $v \in \mathsf L_n$ are trivial. The group $L$ is locally finite. For $G < \mathrm{Aut}(\mathsf T_{\mathbf d})$, we use the group $L_G = \{\gamma \in L : x \cdot \gamma \in x \cdot G \text{ for all } x \in \partial \mathsf T\}$ as an auxiliary group with trivial isotropy groups. In particular if $G$ acts level transitively, then $L$ is used as the auxiliary group.”
--
--   The paper applies this to $G_\omega$ in Example 3.2 (p. 18), “Use the finitary automorphisms $L$ as the auxiliary group. From the definition of the group we have that if $x \notin o \cdot G_\omega$, that is $x$ is not cofinal with $1^\infty$, then the isotropy group $(\mathcal G_\omega)_x$ is trivial.”, where the orbit of $o = 1^\infty$ is identified with its cofinality class. The second conjunct is that identification, for every ray. The words “locally finite” are spelled out, since Mathlib has no such notion for groups.
--
--   The paragraph's general sentences about $L_G$ do not hold for every level-transitive $G$ ([`ErschlerZheng.exists_zpowers_levelTransitive_and_not_isAuxiliary_of_le_finitary`](https://prove2.me/theorems/3d543104-dcca-4724-b3b6-d60c47de1acf)). The statement takes them for $G_\omega$, the case Example 3.2 uses.
-- source:
--   Erschler, A. and Zheng, T., Growth of periodic Grigorchuk groups, Invent. Math. 219 (2020) 1069–1155, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-019-00922-0 (arXiv:1802.09077v2, whose page numbers are used), p. 18, the finitary group L

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ErschlerZheng_Grigorchuk

namespace ErschlerZheng

theorem isLocallyFinite_finitary_and_rightOrbit_eq_and_isAuxiliary :
    (∀ s : Finset Garrido.BinaryTreeAut, (s : Set Garrido.BinaryTreeAut) ⊆ finitary →
      (Subgroup.closure (s : Set Garrido.BinaryTreeAut) : Set Garrido.BinaryTreeAut).Finite) ∧
    (∀ (ω : ℕ → Fin 3) (x : Ray),
      rightOrbit (grigorchuk ω) x = {y : Ray | ∀ᶠ n in Filter.atTop, y n = x n} ∧
        rightOrbit finitary x = {y : Ray | ∀ᶠ n in Filter.atTop, y n = x n}) ∧
    ∀ ω : ℕ → Fin 3, IsAuxiliary (X := Ray) (grigorchuk ω) finitary := by
  sorry

end ErschlerZheng
